(project tuna-math-prims
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: the journaled math prim family - the
      consequence F6 pre-decided (borg/stdlib.borg pre-registered
      failures, OBSERVED 2026-10-03: dictionary-defined arithmetic
      costs 250k v0 steps at 8 BIT and climbs 45x per size doubling;
      the stdlib chapter's own ledger bridge only clears because its
      operands are tiny). This chapter adds math/add, math/sub,
      math/mul, math/cmp, math/neg as journaled host prims over the
      SAME law-5 sign-magnitude ints the stdlib canonized, and moves
      default aggregate arithmetic to them. The review line stands
      verbatim: INVARIANT CHECKS (ledger balance rules, cmp gates)
      stay pure where replay must recompute them; aggregates take
      the prim. Research question, stated so it can be refuted: the
      prim boundary can answer arithmetic in host O(bits) with ZERO
      new journal semantics - same gate tree, same grant row, same
      row schema, same replay - and the only new risk class is
      semantic drift against the in-calculus reference, which a
      differential vector table gates at make time and on every
      prim change forever.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section math-prims
  (doc "The family, its tree law, and why it is SMALL.")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection family
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      math/add, math/sub, math/mul, math/cmp (args tree = 2-list of
      ints), math/neg (args tree = 1-list). Ints are law 5 of
      borg/stdlib.borg conventions: fork of bool sign and LSB-first
      magnitude bit list, canonical (high false bits stripped, both
      zeros = fork false nil = 200 - the SAME tree that is the list
      [f,f] and the pair (false,false); collisions recorded, not
      prevented). Results: add/sub/mul/neg answer the canonical int;
      cmp answers a law-4 small nat (0 = lt, 10 = eq, 110 = gt) so
      tree-case reads it directly.

      JUNK DISCIPLINE (the one place the prim and the stdlib
      diverge by design): the prim reads every bit position that is
      not a canonical bool as 0, emits only canonical forms. The
      seeded stdlib ops PIN a junk-bit passthrough in low positions
      (mag-canonical strips only the top run). That stdlib behavior
      is its own pinned wart; the prim does NOT mirror it. The
      differential law below therefore ranges over the canonical
      domain (every bit a bool); junk inputs are prim-side
      canonicalizations and are probed as such, never presented as
      stdlib equivalence claims. |))))
  (subsection reference-law
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The seeded sabralib defs (int-add, int-sub, int-cmp, int-mul,
      int-neg; borg/stdlib.borg core-v1 manifest) are the REFERENCE
      SEMANTICS. server/lib/math_prims.ml is their host mirror: an
      O(bits) bool-list walk, no width, no OCaml int (size_cap of the
      run already bounds operand trees; the answer is exact at any
      width). Provenance discipline: the mirror ports the ARITHMETIC,
      never the def trees; if a differential vector ever disagrees
      after landing, the report is a book issue (prim contract does
      not bump - contract "2" stays, this family is additive; shapes
      unchanged) and the RECORD of the divergence lands in the
      outcomes below, never a silent shadow fix. |))))
  (subsection zero-new-semantics
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The family introduces no new journal semantics anywhere: the
      gate tree fires by the same call-sites law, the boundary grant
      check is the same live-row lookup (denial is a journaled
      error answer, never an exception - AGENTS 7), the journal row
      is the same schema (args/result ternary under the same
      payload cap, prim_contract "2"), replay re-reads recorded
      answers untouched, deriv seals rows the same way. A math call
      is one journal row and the host side is a pure tree walk:
      the F6 cost class disappears INTO the journal a single row
      at a time. |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. DIFFERENTIAL (the semantic-drift gate): a pinned vector
         table over the canonical domain - mixed signs, both zero
         forms, -0 variants, 1/2/3-bit magnitudes, 64-bit operands -
         prim answer EQUAL to the stdlib def applied to the same
         literals (small sizes via live trees; big sizes via golden
         ternaries pinned by the seeded dictionary at freeze time).
         Any disagreement blocks the landing and, post-landing,
         blocks any prim change (tests/alcotest, pure - no PG).
      2. BOUNDARY: run-boundary run minting math/add computes the
         balance-style aggregate with cost class one-row-per-call;
         journal row carries contract "2"; replay verified;
         grant-missing call answers a journaled denial and the run
         continues (grant checks live per boundary law).
      3. FUEL CONTRAST: the measured host cost of a 64-bit
         math/add call lands next to the F6 table's 11,106,917 v0
         steps - the contrast IS the chapter's reason to exist and
         is recorded as an outcome, not a claim.

      OUTCOMES 2026-10-03 - all three green.
      1. DIFFERENTIAL: tests/math_prim_tests.ml, 12 cases, alcotest
         pure. 30 small vectors (mixed signs, both zero forms, -0
         canonicalization, 1-3 bit magnitudes per op) evaluated
         against the seeded sabra reference IN-TEST (v0, exact);
         64-bit big vectors as golden ternaries (tests/
         math_prim_golden.ml, GENERATED under the reference by
         tools/stdprobe/golden.exe - v0 for add/sub/cmp, v1 sharing
         for int-mul, with the reference step counts in the module
         header as the F6-table confirmation); junk-corner probes
         assert prim-side canonicalization of the recorded split.
         prim == reference on every cell.
      2. BOUNDARY: scripts/verify-9-math-prims.sh green. [9.1]
         math/add (+2)+(-5) -> 2102100 (-3) through the run boundary
         with prim + contract "2" + grant id journaled, replay
         verified; [9.2] no grant -> journaled denial error answer,
         run normal, replay still verifies (journal answers).
      3. FUEL CONTRAST [9.3]: 64-bit math/add journaled wall_ms = 0
         (sub-millisecond host walk) against the pure-v0 11,106,917
         steps of the same operands - the contrast gap is the F6
         thesis paid out one journal row per call. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F7 PRIM DRIFT. The host mirror and the sabra reference can
      diverge silently (junk corner beyond the recorded split, a
      sign-table flip in one but not the other). TRIGGER: any
      differential vector disagrees post-landing. THEN: record in
      FINDINGS.md, the prim is patched to the reference WITH the
      divergence kept visible, and the differential table grows by
      the disagreeing vector. Never a silent prim fix.

      F8 PRIM CREEP. Arithmetic working invites everything else
      (div, mod, decimal, string ops) landing as prims instead of
      conventions. TRIGGER: any prim whose answer a pure def could
      produce under 10k v0 steps at probe scale without a named
      workload under it. THEN: that prim is a book discussion, not
      an implementation; the F6 fuel table is the only justification
      channel that exists. |)))))
)
